ge:**\n   - The smallest multiple of 7 greater than or equal to 100 can be found by dividing 100 by 7 and rounding up to the nearest whole number.\n     \\[\n     \\frac{100}{7} \\approx 14.2857 \\implies \\lceil 14.2857 \\rceil = 15\n     \\]\n     So, the smallest multiple of 7 is \\( 7 \\times 15 = 105 \\).\n\n   - The largest multiple of 7 less than or equal to 200 can be found by dividing 200 by 7 and rounding down to the nearest whole number.\n     \\[\n     \\frac{200}{7} \\approx 28.5714 \\implies \\lfloor 28.5714 \\rfloor = 28\n     \\]\n     So, the largest multiple of 7 is \\( 7 \\times 28 = 196 \\).\n\n2. **List the multiples of 7 between 100 and 200:**\n   The multiples of 7 between 105 and 196 are:\n   \\[\n   105, 112, 119, \\ldots, 196\n   \\]\n   This is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\( a = 105 \\) and the common difference \\( d = 7 \\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"972c9ebf296ffe0585bdcb0c6db63244e401d2c5ba321ff55ee760f0297db88c"},"seconds":25.009724378585815,"seed":236141494884880862,"stop":"eos","text":"3. **Find the number of terms in the sequence:**\n   The general form of the nth term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   a_n = a + (n-1)d\n   \\]\n   To find the number of terms, we set \\( a_n = 196 \\) and solve for \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   196 = 105 + (n-1) \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   196 - 105 = (n-1) \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   91 = (n-1) \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n-1 = \\frac{91}{7} = 13\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n = 14\n   \\]\n   So, there are 14 terms in the sequence.\n\n4. **Calculate the sum of the arithmetic sequence:**\n   The sum \\( S_n \\) of the first \\( n \\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   S_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\times (a + l)\n   \\]\n   where \\( a \\) is the first term, \\( l \\) is the last term, and \\( n \\) is the number of terms.\n   \\[\n   S_{14} = \\frac{14}{2} \\times (105 + 196)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_{14} = 7 \\times 201\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_{14} = 1407\n   \\]\n\nThe sum of all the multiples of 7 between 100 and 200 is \\boxed{1407}."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1407}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"3. **Find the number of terms in the sequence:**\n   The general form of the nth term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   a_n = a + (n-1)d\n   \\]\n   To find the number of terms, we set \\( a_n = 196 \\) and solve for \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   196 = 105 + (n-1) \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   196 - 105 = (n-1) \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   91 = (n-1) \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n-1 = \\frac{91}{7} = 13\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n = 14\n   \\]\n   So, there are 14 terms in the sequence.\n\n4. **Calculate the sum of the arithmetic sequence:**\n   The sum \\( S_n \\) of the first \\( n \\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   S_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\times (a + l)\n   \\]\n   where \\( a \\) is the first term, \\( l \\) is the last term, and \\( n \\) is the number of terms.\n   \\[\n   S_{14} = \\frac{14}{2} \\times (105 + 196)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_{14} = 7 \\times 201\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_{14} = 1407\n   \\]\n\nThe sum of all the multiples of 7 between 100 and 200 is \\boxed{1407}.","id":"algebra:748"}
